{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>The meter has to survive the loop that's breaking the budget</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      The cost meter and the cap have to live outside the thing they're watching. If the counter only updates after the agent's loop finishes its turn, a loop that never finishes a turn never updates the counter either — track and check the budget between individual tool calls, not just between full agent turns.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Decide up front what "graceful" means for your product before you're debugging it live: a downgrade to a cheaper model changes response quality, a hard block ends the interaction. Both are better than a silent overrun, but which one fires at which threshold is a product decision worth writing down, not improvising during an incident.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Enforce the cap server-side, not just in the UI — a client-rendered meter that stops updating is a display bug, not a spend control, if the backend will still happily process the next tool call. The UI meter is for visibility and trust; the actual budget enforcement needs to be a check the server makes before it spends money, independent of whether the tab is even open.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

